83
00:07:39,824 --> 00:07:50,468
Um here's the mental model. Evals are testing for AI, and traces are logs for AI. Why we had to come up with two completely new names for those things, I don't really know.

84
00:07:52,014 --> 00:07:53,424
you write tests for your code.

85
00:07:53,710 --> 00:07:55,664
Given this input, I expect this output.

86
00:07:55,984 --> 00:08:02,020
evals do the same thing, but for AI outputs where the outputs are never exactly the same twice.

87
00:08:02,510 --> 00:08:14,084
Just as logs record what your server did at runtime, traces record what your AI did. So every agent call, every tool call, every LLM invocation with the inputs and outputs at each step.

88
00:08:15,342 --> 00:08:18,532
The building blocks of a trace are called spans.

89
00:08:20,206 --> 00:08:25,348
in this little screenshot, you can see a trace in a rise AX. It's made up of a bunch of spans, it's got this tree structure.

90
00:08:26,544 --> 00:08:34,724
and each span represents one step in the execution. An LLM call is a span, a tool call is a span, a full agent turn is a span that contains other spans inside of it.

91
00:08:35,406 --> 00:08:40,784
Each span records its input, its output, timing, token counts, and a whole bunch of metadata.

92
00:08:41,184 --> 00:08:43,664
everything that you need to understand what happened at that step.

93
00:08:43,984 --> 00:08:47,584
Traces tell you what happened, evals tell you whether it was any good.

94
00:08:47,790 --> 00:08:48,224
That is the

95
00:08:49,024 --> 00:08:50,304
mental model for today.

96
00:08:50,544 --> 00:08:52,004
This is not rocket science.

97
00:08:54,126 --> 00:08:59,108
But let me talk about why we even need evals, and that is the vibes problem.

98
00:09:01,486 --> 00:09:07,184
AI features usually get shipped by building an AI feature, running a few queries, saying, does this look right?

99
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:13,796
Yeah, it looks good, and then you ship it. That is by far the most popular method of shipping AI applications today.

100
00:09:14,958 --> 00:09:18,212
And the problem is that then it fails on inputs that you didn't test.

101
00:09:18,864 --> 00:09:25,220
And the thing is, it probably looks good on your test queries, but the problem is that you ran it three times. Three times is not a test suite.

102
00:09:26,670 --> 00:09:33,344
The usual fix for flaky software is unit tests, and they don't work here because the output is non-deterministic.

103
00:09:33,824 --> 00:09:41,184
The same prompt produces different text every run, so two different responses might both be correct, so there's no expected string that you can assert

104
00:09:41,184 --> 00:09:46,372
against. So teams fall back on human review. They watch it run, it looks fine, and they ship it.

105
00:09:47,344 --> 00:09:51,972
That doesn't scale, it doesn't catch regressions, and most importantly, it doesn't run in CI.

106
00:09:54,286 --> 00:10:00,196
Here's some things you can't do without evals. if you change your system prompt to fix a tone issue, then the tone would get better.

107
00:10:00,464 --> 00:10:03,344
But now your bot is hallucinating product features.

108
00:10:03,424 --> 00:10:07,396
Without evals, you wouldn't catch that until a user reports it.

109
00:10:08,110 --> 00:10:11,716
With a faithfulness eval, you would have seen the spike before it shipped.

110
00:10:12,624 --> 00:10:17,264
And every prompt potentially affects every kind of input that users send.

111
00:10:17,358 --> 00:10:25,344
So you can be changing the prompt for one thing and have a completely unexpected effect at some totally different part of your application because the prompt is just a huge block

112
00:10:25,344 --> 00:10:26,980
of text that you're sending to your AI.

113
00:10:28,224 --> 00:10:32,864
You can't manually test every combination. So without evals, you're playing whack-a-mole.

114
00:10:32,974 --> 00:10:35,332
You fix one problem and you break something somewhere else.

115
00:10:36,014 --> 00:10:40,100
Evals instead give you a number that you can track and compare and act on.

116
00:10:41,838 --> 00:10:46,692
You also can't switch models without evals, and this matters more and more because this field moves so fast.

117
00:10:47,344 --> 00:10:49,284
new models drop every few months.

118
00:10:51,086 --> 00:10:57,344
without eval, switching models means weeks of manual testing. Is it better? Is it worse? Did something get did something break? You don't know.

119
00:10:57,742 --> 00:11:06,532
With evals, you run the suite, you compare the scores, and you know within hours, and that is the difference between flying by the seat of your pants and flying by instruments.

120
00:11:08,270 --> 00:11:09,796
And this is not theoretical.

121
00:11:10,224 --> 00:11:19,044
real teams, Descript, bolt, anthropic's own clawed code all follow the same arc of shipping on vibes and then discovering that vibes don't scale,

122
00:11:19,664 --> 00:11:25,344
and then building evals. And that is the same arc that we're going to follow in today's workshop.

123
00:11:27,214 --> 00:11:30,064
As I mentioned earlier, there are two broad types of evals.

124
00:11:30,382 --> 00:11:32,384
Code evals are deterministic functions.

125
00:11:32,558 --> 00:11:38,944
They run in milliseconds and they cost nothing, and they give you an immediate unambiguous answer, like did the output parses JSON?

126
00:11:39,184 --> 00:11:41,264
Is the output less than 500 tokens?

127
00:11:41,486 --> 00:11:43,332
Did it mention the thing that I asked about?

128
00:11:43,854 --> 00:11:48,624
The big advantage of code evals is that they are fast and cheap and totally reproducible.

129
00:11:49,550 --> 00:11:52,164
They get the same input and they give the same answer every time.

130
00:11:52,462 --> 00:11:54,916
That makes them very similar to old style unit tests.

131
00:11:55,438 --> 00:11:58,724
The downside is that, like old style unit tests, they can be brittle.

132
00:11:59,344 --> 00:12:03,012
We have some strategies that can help avoid that, and I'm going to be covering those later.

133
00:12:05,038 --> 00:12:12,548
Because two perfectly correct answers might be worded completely differently, and you have to adjust your expectations of unit tests to be able to handle that.

134
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:25,104
The second type of eval is an LLM as a judge eval. These use a second LLM to grade your outputs against a rubric, rubric being yet another word that we made up that just means rules.

135
00:12:25,390 --> 00:12:29,092
It is your set of rules that you put in your prompt about what counts as good.

136
00:12:30,478 --> 00:12:32,292
LLM evals are much more flexible.

137
00:12:32,784 --> 00:12:35,812
They can handle the kinds of questions that code evals can't answer.

138
00:12:36,464 --> 00:12:38,944
Is this response factually accurate?

139
00:12:39,278 --> 00:12:42,116
Did it stay faithful to the source material? Is the tone right?

140
00:12:42,544 --> 00:12:50,404
For a customer service context, the strength of LLM judges is that they understand the meaning, not just the strings that are in your output.

141
00:12:51,566 --> 00:12:53,744
But LLM judges have trade offs.

142
00:12:53,904 --> 00:12:58,116
The weakness is that they cost money to run and they are non deterministic themselves.

143
00:12:59,264 --> 00:13:04,548
So they can be wrong, which means that you need to calibrate them against human judgment, and we're going to do that later today.

144
00:13:06,350 --> 00:13:09,700
There's also a third type of eval, which is human evaluation.

145
00:13:10,432 --> 00:13:15,460
A domain expert can read your output and grade it, and that is the gold standard for quality.

146
00:13:16,112 --> 00:13:21,028
But it is slow and expensive, and humans get tired, which is why we can't use it all the time.

147
00:13:22,382 --> 00:13:27,552
Fun fact that's not very fun: human annotators miss up to 50% of defects due to fatigue.

148
00:13:27,632 --> 00:13:29,060
So they're not perfect either.

149
00:13:29,312 --> 00:13:38,592
So when you build an LLM as a judge and you're like, well, it doesn't always catch my errors. Remember that if you were judging with a human, the human would also not always catch your errors.

150
00:13:39,472 --> 00:13:41,312
But these are complementary approaches.

151
00:13:41,472 --> 00:13:43,632
Most real applications are going to use a

152
00:13:44,654 --> 00:13:48,292
collection of code evals, LLM as a judge, and human evaluation.

153
00:13:49,358 --> 00:13:50,912
So when do you use which?

154
00:13:51,406 --> 00:13:54,180
Use code evals when the answer is deterministic.

155
00:13:54,512 --> 00:13:59,152
So format validation, length limits, forbidden phrases, required fields.

156
00:13:59,312 --> 00:14:06,272
Use LLM judges when you need semantic understanding. A correctness eval asks, did it answer the question accurately?

157
00:14:06,542 --> 00:14:10,564
A faithfulness eval asks, did it stick to the source documents without hallucinating?

158
00:14:11,822 --> 00:14:15,588
And you should keep humans in the loop for failure modes that you haven't seen before.

159
00:14:16,192 --> 00:14:19,300
And to verify that your LLM judges are actually judging correctly.

160
00:14:19,472 --> 00:14:21,028
LLM judges can be wrong.

161
00:14:21,792 --> 00:14:25,220
Judging them against human judgment is how you know whether or not you can trust them.

162
00:14:27,552 --> 00:14:35,524
All of that is true of any application that uses LLMs, but agents make things even harder in some ways that are worth calling out.

163
00:14:35,790 --> 00:14:37,924
You can think of it as a ladder of complexity.

164
00:14:38,318 --> 00:14:42,272
A single LLM call is relatively simple. You get an input, you get an output.

165
00:14:42,510 --> 00:14:51,312
When you are talking about an agent, an agent is making a series of tool calls and a series of decisions, which means that at every single step in the chain,

166
00:14:52,270 --> 00:14:59,392
the chances that it has made a mistake are getting higher, and so the chances that it will go off the rails are going to get greater.

167
00:14:59,950 --> 00:15:09,472
So you have to judge the intermediate steps as well. You have to check whether your agent is using the right tool, did it pass the right arguments, did it know when to stop?

168
00:15:09,806 --> 00:15:13,152
Getting stuck in loops is a real failure case for agents.

169
00:15:13,792 --> 00:15:18,468
And then multi-agent systems, if you are building those, add yet another level of complexity.

170
00:15:18,672 --> 00:15:24,032
They add handoffs. One agent decides to pass work to another. Did the triage agent route correctly?

171
00:15:24,238 --> 00:15:27,472
Did the specialist agent handle the handoff gracefully?

172
00:15:27,632 --> 00:15:34,148
Each layer adds new ways that things go wrong, and non-determinism means that you need to have more evals

173
00:15:34,862 --> 00:15:36,548
because errors can cascade.

174
00:15:38,272 --> 00:15:41,572
Here's what an example of a cascading failure looks like.

175
00:15:41,872 --> 00:15:50,272
If you ask a query about Tesla, meaning the car company, your agent searches the web, finds a whole bunch of information about an 18th-century inventor,

176
00:15:50,432 --> 00:15:57,124
passes that back to your agent, which writes a beautiful, well-formatted report about an 18th-century inventor, and passes that to your boss.

177
00:15:58,190 --> 00:15:59,952
Nothing that it did there was wrong.

178
00:16:00,352 --> 00:16:07,588
You told it to search the web for Tesla, it searched the web for Tesla, it got information about Tesla, and it wrote a report about Tesla. Where did it go wrong?

179
00:16:09,774 --> 00:16:14,512
This is worse than an obvious failure because you have to be reading your inputs and outputs for that to work.

180
00:16:14,672 --> 00:16:17,092
You can't, there was no unit test that would have caught that.

181
00:16:20,032 --> 00:16:26,512
But agents can also do the opposite. They can get things right in a way that your tests weren't expecting and get graded wrong as a result.

182
00:16:26,672 --> 00:16:33,764
This happened to Anthropic when they ran a benchmark called Tau Squared Bench, which simulates multi-turn customer service tasks.

183
00:16:35,246 --> 00:16:44,516
They asked the agent to book flights and asked it to do something that they thought was impossible, which was reschedule an economy class flight.

184
00:16:45,072 --> 00:16:56,964
But it turned out that the policies that they gave the agent allowed the agent to first upgrade the flight to first class. First class flights can be rescheduled, which economy flights cannot.

185
00:16:57,166 --> 00:17:00,592
So the agent found a way to reschedule an economy flight

186
00:17:02,574 --> 00:17:06,436
failed the test because it was supposed to be not able to do that, and it was able to do that.

187
00:17:06,992 --> 00:17:14,244
So this is an example of how agents being flexible, agents finding things that you didn't think of need to be accounted for in your tests.

188
00:17:16,462 --> 00:17:22,276
There is a difference between creatively correct and wrong, and we're going to come back to that when we talk about grading your agents later.

189
00:17:23,086 --> 00:17:24,976
Or rather, grading your evals.

190
00:17:25,856 --> 00:17:38,852
There's also a second way of categorizing your evals into two categories, which is capability evals and regression evals. A capability eval asks, can my agent do this thing at all?

191
00:17:40,334 --> 00:17:47,456
Capability evals are expected to mostly fail. Your agent is expected to get a very low score on them because they give you a hill to climb.

192
00:17:47,536 --> 00:17:49,956
It gives you something for your agent to try and get better at.

193
00:17:50,736 --> 00:18:01,764
Once you've climbed that hill, your capability eval turns into a regression eval. A regression eval is an eval that you expect your agent to pass completely or nearly completely every single time.

194
00:18:02,256 --> 00:18:12,736
So as your agent matures, you will be giving it capability eval after capability eval and slowly turning them into a suite of regression evals that you run all the time to make sure that

195
00:18:13,390 --> 00:18:15,012
previous behavior has not regressed.

196
00:18:17,550 --> 00:18:19,856
This is what an eval result looks like.

197
00:18:20,176 --> 00:18:29,124
Every eval produces a score and a label. In this case, it's a numeric score, just often just zero or one. Binary scores are very easy for LLMs to do.

198
00:18:30,766 --> 00:18:34,308
And it comes with a human readable label like correct or incorrect or valid or invalid.

199
00:18:35,406 --> 00:18:38,212
LLM judges add a third thing, which is the explanation.

