Before you write a single evaluator, start with your data.

396
00:39:04,976 --> 00:39:10,372
Read a dozen or more of your traces end to end. This is the most important practice in this entire workshop.

397
00:39:11,136 --> 00:39:13,456
Focus on the ones where something went wrong.

398
00:39:13,646 --> 00:39:15,364
What was the input? What was the output?

399
00:39:15,616 --> 00:39:17,188
What specifically is broken?

400
00:39:17,806 --> 00:39:20,900
This sounds extremely old-fashioned, just read the input.

401
00:39:21,870 --> 00:39:25,060
But it is one of the highest value activities in agent development.

402
00:39:25,456 --> 00:39:33,380
Anthropic, for instance, invested in tooling specifically for viewing eval transcripts, and their team spends a whole lot of time just looking at eval transcripts every day.

403
00:39:35,374 --> 00:39:40,388
A trace tells you whether the agent made a genuine mistake or whether your graders rejected a valid solution.

404
00:39:41,358 --> 00:39:49,696
If you automate before you understand your failures, then you're going to create an eval that measures what's easy to measure instead of what actually matters.

405
00:39:53,934 --> 00:39:55,456
You need requirements first.

406
00:39:55,616 --> 00:40:02,788
Before you can categorize failures, you need to know what success looks like. You can't say it doesn't work if you haven't defined what it works means.

407
00:40:03,616 --> 00:40:06,916
For our financial analyst, what does a good report look like?

408
00:40:07,616 --> 00:40:15,616
It should reference the correct ticker, it should include some real, recent, actionable financial data and include actionable recommendations.

409
00:40:15,696 --> 00:40:25,444
It should distinguish between forward-looking analysis and historical summary. Those are our success criteria, and they seem obvious when you write them down, but lots of teams never do.

410
00:40:26,176 --> 00:40:29,956
They ship an agent and then react to complaints instead of defining the bar up front.

411
00:40:31,950 --> 00:40:39,044
Writing requirements down turns vague disappointment into specific testable criteria, and those criteria are what turn into your evals.

412
00:40:40,558 --> 00:40:48,656
Here's the thing about defining success that it's important to note though, which is that it is not something that your engineers can do, or not something your engineers can do alone.

413
00:40:48,846 --> 00:40:56,932
The definition of good usually lives in the domain knowledge, and the domain knowledge lives in the people that your company will often laughably refer to as non-technical.

414
00:40:57,550 --> 00:41:03,776
So your products managers, your QA, your support team, those are the people who know what the definition of good really is.

415
00:41:03,936 --> 00:41:06,016
They know what the failures are going to look like.

416
00:41:06,254 --> 00:41:08,772
Those are the people who should be helping you write your evals.

417
00:41:10,478 --> 00:41:14,532
OpenAI put it really nicely. They said that people management skills are AI skills.

418
00:41:14,656 --> 00:41:23,044
So clear goals, direct feedback, knowing what your value proposition is, those skills matter more than ever when the system is probabilistic.

419
00:41:23,936 --> 00:41:26,176
And this is one of the things that AX is built around.

420
00:41:26,256 --> 00:41:33,456
Anyone on your team can read traces or add annotations and contribute to your criteria. This is not just an engineering surface.

421
00:41:33,616 --> 00:41:36,260
You're expected to have other members of your team in here.

422
00:41:38,350 --> 00:41:40,452
A quick note on where to get this test data.

423
00:41:41,710 --> 00:41:47,812
We have these traces because we already ran the agent ourselves, but what if you're building something new and you don't have real traffic yet?

424
00:41:48,224 --> 00:41:50,944
You can do what we just did, which is you can use synthetic data.

425
00:41:51,150 --> 00:41:55,236
You can have an LLM generate diverse queries across your expected categories.

426
00:41:55,950 --> 00:41:59,584
So research Tesla financial report performance is one phrasing.

427
00:41:59,824 --> 00:42:03,344
What's going on with Tesla stock is another query that's asking the same thing.

428
00:42:03,630 --> 00:42:07,972
Yo, is Tesla a buy right now? Is a third way of expressing the same query.

429
00:42:08,464 --> 00:42:10,784
They're all the same intent, but they look very different.

430
00:42:10,894 --> 00:42:14,564
So vary the phrasing, the complexity, the level of specificity.

431
00:42:15,184 --> 00:42:24,544
A domain expert should review your set of queries because LLM-generated queries tend to cluster around obvious phrasings and miss the weird stuff that real users

432
00:42:24,544 --> 00:42:28,304
write. You also need to include edge cases in your test data.

433
00:42:28,398 --> 00:42:32,036
So things like non-existent tickers, multi-part questions, jailbreak attempts.

434
00:42:33,518 --> 00:42:39,204
Those might be 1% of your traffic, but they are the 1% of your traffic that ends up being a PR disaster in the press.

435
00:42:39,598 --> 00:42:43,844
Your test data should look like production data, not what you wish production looked like.

436
00:42:44,544 --> 00:42:50,052
So synthetic data can get you started, but as soon as you have production data, production data should be what you're running on.

