467
00:45:37,710 --> 00:45:42,980
There is a structured way to do this reading, which as I mentioned earlier is called coding.

468
00:45:43,246 --> 00:45:45,540
There's open coding and axial coding.

469
00:45:46,606 --> 00:45:51,872
And it comes from qualitative research. Open coding means you read each trace and you write down what you see.

470
00:45:51,952 --> 00:45:56,516
So stuff like vague recommendation, made up a number, talked about the wrong ticker.

471
00:45:57,614 --> 00:45:58,912
You're not trying to be neat.

472
00:45:58,992 --> 00:46:07,236
As a programmer, it is very tempting to try and come up with categories in advance and say, oh, this is a retrieval failure or whatever.

473
00:46:07,872 --> 00:46:10,020
Don't do that when you're doing open coding.

474
00:46:10,192 --> 00:46:13,508
Just write down what the problem was in as natural language as possible.

475
00:46:15,472 --> 00:46:15,792
And

476
00:46:17,072 --> 00:46:23,152
then when you find the failure, sorry, and then you want to do Axial coding afterwards.

477
00:46:23,438 --> 00:46:29,472
Axial coding is when you look at all of the open codes that you've put together and then you do the categorization.

478
00:46:29,646 --> 00:46:37,152
You say, okay, now there are five things that look similar. I'm going to give them all a single category and turn them into categories.

479
00:46:39,822 --> 00:46:43,844
The second pass, the axial coding pass is structural.

480
00:46:44,432 --> 00:46:45,232
and like I said,

481
00:46:46,032 --> 00:46:50,852
the tendency, the temptation is to try and go straight to Axial coding, and you should resist that.

482
00:46:51,662 --> 00:46:54,592
When you find a failure, you should ask why it failed.

483
00:46:54,734 --> 00:46:56,752
So the response was wrong, is a symptom.

484
00:46:56,912 --> 00:46:58,436
Did it get bad search results?

485
00:46:58,702 --> 00:47:04,512
That is a retrieval failure. Did it get the right data but the wrong conclusion? Then that is a reasoning error.

486
00:47:04,878 --> 00:47:07,312
Did it make up a stock price? That's obviously hallucination.

487
00:47:07,534 --> 00:47:10,592
Did it answer outside of its domain? That is a scope violation.

488
00:47:10,752 --> 00:47:13,712
Did it do something that you didn't tell it that it should be able to do?

489
00:47:13,952 --> 00:47:18,276
Each root cause, each Axial code, points to a different fix.

490
00:47:20,142 --> 00:47:22,180
if you don't know the cause, you can't pick the right remedy.

491
00:47:23,534 --> 00:47:24,868
Once you've categorized,

492
00:47:26,990 --> 00:47:28,112
which I did here,

493
00:47:30,382 --> 00:47:32,512
you can sum them up into

494
00:47:35,406 --> 00:47:36,004
a table.

495
00:47:37,742 --> 00:47:38,032
So

496
00:47:41,312 --> 00:47:46,244
confession time, I didn't actually read all of those traces and code them, I just gave them random Axial codes.

497
00:47:46,912 --> 00:47:50,832
but what you end up with is this table of root cause frequency.

498
00:47:50,912 --> 00:47:55,908
Things like looks good, possible hallucination, reasoning gap, unverifiable data, missing recommendation.

499
00:47:56,334 --> 00:48:02,372
It's tempting to look at the top one, the possible hallucination, and decide that that is the most important one to fix.

500
00:48:02,912 --> 00:48:04,192
But in reality,

501
00:48:05,262 --> 00:48:06,672
you're going to want to

502
00:48:08,654 --> 00:48:11,972
balance between frequency and severity.

503
00:48:12,494 --> 00:48:20,164
So if one time in 100, instead of doing a financial report, it gives the user instructions on how to make a bomb, you fix that one first,

504
00:48:20,782 --> 00:48:23,300
because that is the most severe possible failure.

505
00:48:24,032 --> 00:48:29,956
so you have to multiply the severity by the frequency to get to which ones you decide to fix first.

506
00:48:31,630 --> 00:48:34,180
and that is why you look at the data before you write your evals.

507
00:48:36,910 --> 00:48:40,292
one more concept before we get started on that is the Swiss cheese model,

508
00:48:41,232 --> 00:48:43,232
which is a concept from safety engineering.

509
00:48:43,632 --> 00:48:47,472
Imagine each layer of defense as a slice of Swiss cheese.

510
00:48:47,552 --> 00:48:51,076
Each slice has holes, gaps where problems can slip through.

511
00:48:51,472 --> 00:48:54,112
But if you stack enough slices, the holes don't line up.

512
00:48:54,318 --> 00:48:56,612
So one gets through one layer gets caught by the next.

513
00:48:56,992 --> 00:49:00,112
your code eval catches format issues but misses semantic problems.

514
00:49:00,192 --> 00:49:08,132
Your LLM judge catches reasoning gaps but misses subtle hallucinations, and your human review catches the subtle stuff, but it can't scale to every trace.

515
00:49:08,512 --> 00:49:14,340
No single eval in that set catches everything, and the combination is what gives you coverage.

516
00:49:16,398 --> 00:49:17,924
so stack the cheese.

