603
00:57:34,320 --> 00:57:35,760
I referred to this earlier.

604
00:57:36,046 --> 00:57:39,652
There's a common instinct to check that the agent followed a specific sequence of steps.

605
00:57:40,046 --> 00:57:43,428
Did it call this tool first and then that tool in that order?

606
00:57:44,400 --> 00:57:48,484
And practitioners have found this to be, in practice, too rigid.

607
00:57:48,960 --> 00:57:54,180
agents regularly find valid approaches that the eval designer didn't anticipate.

608
00:57:55,040 --> 00:57:55,360
so

609
00:57:56,462 --> 00:57:57,924
if you grade the path,

610
00:57:59,214 --> 00:58:00,388
you punish creativity.

611
00:58:00,590 --> 00:58:02,372
Instead, grade what the agent produced.

612
00:58:02,560 --> 00:58:04,160
Did the output have the right information?

613
00:58:04,494 --> 00:58:06,640
Did the final state match what you wanted?

614
00:58:07,040 --> 00:58:12,004
our ticker check doesn't care how the agent found the data, it just checks is the ticker in the output?

615
00:58:12,640 --> 00:58:15,524
That is the right level of abstraction for most code evals.

616
00:58:17,550 --> 00:58:22,500
So now let's get to built-in evals, LLM evals. This is where it begins to get more interesting.

617
00:58:23,680 --> 00:58:27,920
code can check whether the ticker appears, but it can't check whether the analysis is good.

618
00:58:28,110 --> 00:58:30,720
Is the financial data accurate? Is the report complete?

619
00:58:30,880 --> 00:58:32,548
Are the recommendations well reasoned?

620
00:58:33,120 --> 00:58:36,740
These are semantic questions, and for semantic questions you need a judge.

621
00:58:37,518 --> 00:58:40,484
Every LLM as a judge has three parts.

622
00:58:40,910 --> 00:58:44,676
It has a judge model, which is the LLM that is doing the grading for you.

623
00:58:45,070 --> 00:58:53,220
It has a prompt template, which, as I mentioned, is also called a rubric, which is the criteria that the judge applies, the rules of what defines good.

624
00:58:53,760 --> 00:58:56,560
And it has the data, which is the examples being evaluated.

625
00:58:56,814 --> 00:58:59,908
AX keeps these three things separate, which means that you can mix and match.

626
00:59:00,014 --> 00:59:04,516
You can try the same criteria with different judge models, the same judge model with different criteria.

627
00:59:04,960 --> 00:59:06,500
It is modular by design.

628
00:59:07,200 --> 00:59:13,316
The good news is that AX ships with a bunch of built-in evals, so you don't have to write your own.

629
00:59:13,710 --> 00:59:15,360
There are a bunch of types of evals

630
00:59:17,678 --> 00:59:25,604
that are often going to apply to most AI applications, so we've already written the prompts for those, and you don't have to write them yourself.

631
00:59:26,670 --> 00:59:29,840
Correctness, for instance, checks whether a response is factually accurate.

632
00:59:30,126 --> 00:59:39,840
Faithfulness checks whether the response stays grounded in the source material, and there are evals for tool selection. Did the agent pick the right tool and tool invocation?

633
00:59:40,110 --> 00:59:42,080
Did it pass the right arguments to that tool?

634
00:59:42,478 --> 00:59:49,444
Stuff like document relevance, refusal detection, many of the things that you'd want to check in an agentic application come out of the box.

635
00:59:51,694 --> 00:59:57,152
So the first eval that we're going to run is a correctness eval.

636
01:00:00,686 --> 01:00:02,436
And spoiler alert, it's not going to work.

637
01:00:05,006 --> 01:00:06,272
Setting it up is very easy.

638
01:00:06,432 --> 01:00:16,352
We give it an LLM. In this case, I've chosen the anthropic LLM, Sonnet 4.6, because it is better than Haiku, which is the thing that is doing the work.

639
01:00:16,622 --> 01:00:19,460
You often want to pick a bigger, slower model to be your judge.

640
01:00:20,558 --> 01:00:25,892
And then I've just given correctness evaluated the LLM and

641
01:00:26,830 --> 01:00:28,068
run it right here.

642
01:00:35,214 --> 01:00:35,787
Like I said,

643
01:00:37,422 --> 01:00:46,592
Once we've run the evaluation with evaluate data frame, we've also turned on suppress tracing here so that our evaluation doesn't send a bunch of extra traces to

644
01:00:46,832 --> 01:00:47,460
AX.

645
01:00:49,518 --> 01:00:52,484
It gives us this output, which is a bunch of evaluation rows.

646
01:00:55,182 --> 01:00:58,272
These are not very readable. This is why you need AX to look at them.

647
01:00:59,982 --> 01:01:03,396
The judge reads the input and output from each span and grades it against the rubric.

648
01:01:07,086 --> 01:01:11,652
And we can see that every score is zero. If I scroll across

649
01:01:15,854 --> 01:01:19,076
status completed, name correctness, score zero.

650
01:01:19,502 --> 01:01:29,312
The reason it's doing this is because correctness is judging against the LLM's definition of what is correct and what is true,

651
01:01:29,632 --> 01:01:33,572
which means that it is relying on the LLM's training data.

652
01:01:34,126 --> 01:01:42,112
What we're doing in this agent is a whole bunch of live web search to get current stock prices and up-to-date financial information,

653
01:01:42,272 --> 01:01:45,892
which means the agent is talking about things that happened in 2026,

654
01:01:46,766 --> 01:01:50,788
but the LLM that's doing the judging only has information that stops in January.

655
01:01:51,054 --> 01:01:54,432
So if you read the explanations of our correctness eval

656
01:01:57,486 --> 01:02:02,052
inside of AX, you can scroll to evaluations, you can look at correctness, you can say that it is incorrect.

657
01:02:03,310 --> 01:02:10,660
I will save you a bunch of reading of a very densely packed column. What it's doing is complaining that there's a bunch of stuff that comes from the future that it couldn't possibly know.

658
01:02:11,950 --> 01:02:18,756
So our correctness eval here has completely failed. This is not what you wanted to do. So instead, we are going to use a faithfulness eval.

659
01:02:20,590 --> 01:02:25,552
Faithfulness is a better eval for our use case because the judge actually has context.

660
01:02:25,646 --> 01:02:33,712
In faithfulness, you give the LLM as a judge the information that it is being based on, and then say,

661
01:02:33,872 --> 01:02:39,684
based on the same information that the judge that my agent collected, did it do a good job of writing the report?

662
01:02:39,952 --> 01:02:50,832
This is this way they are on a level playing field. They both have the same amount of information, and your slow, complicated judge agent can judge whether your quick, fast worker agent

663
01:02:51,854 --> 01:02:52,112
was

664
01:02:52,992 --> 01:02:54,116
doing a good job.

665
01:02:56,782 --> 01:02:58,212
so let's look at the faithfulness

666
01:02:58,926 --> 01:02:59,268
cells.

667
01:03:00,526 --> 01:03:01,764
just like everything else,

668
01:03:02,512 --> 01:03:04,068
these can be done

669
01:03:05,486 --> 01:03:07,076
online as well as offline.

670
01:03:07,472 --> 01:03:11,076
So there is a faithfulness built-in evaluator,

671
01:03:13,262 --> 01:03:14,192
which isn't in this view.

672
01:03:14,414 --> 01:03:21,604
There is a faithfulness built-in evaluator which you can pull up and you can give your input, output, and your context to it.

673
01:03:23,054 --> 01:03:27,632
again, you can do this in code as well, and if you're using the skills, this is probably what's going to happen.

674
01:03:27,792 --> 01:03:33,956
Your skills will say, Oh, I'm not going to use the UI, I'm going to use the code, which is why I show it here.

675
01:03:35,630 --> 01:03:39,312
so the first thing we do is we extract the research context from the traces.

676
01:03:39,598 --> 01:03:48,996
As you'll remember, we do a two-step process. So I take the output from the first step where it does the research, and I attach that to all of my spans

677
01:03:50,574 --> 01:03:50,832
as

678
01:03:51,712 --> 01:03:55,012
the context for the judge to work on.

679
01:03:55,792 --> 01:03:57,860
and then I run the faithfulness evaluator.

680
01:03:58,592 --> 01:03:58,912
So

681
01:04:01,070 --> 01:04:09,312
here you can see spans with context. These are the same spans that I just pulled out of AX. I attached at the column level all of my context, and then

682
01:04:10,510 --> 01:04:20,772
I called evaluate data frame with those spans and I gave it my faithfulness evaluator, which I instantiated here just like I instantiated the correctness evaluator.

683
01:04:21,712 --> 01:04:25,252
This gives us much more interesting data.

684
01:04:25,582 --> 01:04:28,772
This gives us unfaithful seven and faithful six.

685
01:04:31,392 --> 01:04:31,712
so

686
01:04:33,550 --> 01:04:38,592
roughly half the time, my very simple report is hallucinating.

687
01:04:38,734 --> 01:04:42,788
It is coming up with things that were not in the source data according to Sonnet.

688
01:04:45,582 --> 01:04:48,000
And that is, this is a useful eval.

689
01:04:48,270 --> 01:04:56,484
This is an eval where we're failing half the time. This is a capability eval where we can climb the hill, modify our prompt, and get it better at writing a grounded report.

690
01:04:56,846 --> 01:04:58,884
This is exactly the kind of eval that you want.

691
01:05:04,238 --> 01:05:10,720
So that's two built-in evals and two very different signals, and there's a really important lesson here, which is correctness gave us zero out of 13,

692
01:05:10,800 --> 01:05:15,840
and faithfulness told us that reports aren't grounded in their sources roughly half the time.

693
01:05:16,110 --> 01:05:20,560
The difference isn't that one eval is better than the other, the difference is the use case.

694
01:05:20,654 --> 01:05:26,212
There are lots of use cases where that correctness eval that I showed you earlier works just fine for some use cases.

695
01:05:27,278 --> 01:05:31,364
it's just not in this specific use case where we're doing live real-time research.

696
01:05:33,006 --> 01:05:35,492
So it's important to know the question that you're actually asking.

697
01:05:38,926 --> 01:05:40,400
Built-in evals are your starting point.

698
01:05:40,480 --> 01:05:48,452
They give you an immediate signal without any prompt engineering, but even with a good built-in like faithfulness, there are a bunch of things that it can't check.

699
01:05:50,542 --> 01:05:56,004
our financial analyst should produce actionable recommendations, it shouldn't just summarize data.

700
01:05:57,742 --> 01:06:04,160
So not just you know give you a financial report, but tell you what to do with the financial report. Should I buy, sell, or hold?

701
01:06:05,582 --> 01:06:13,540
no built-in template checks for that. This AX does not cover every single use case, so you have to build a specific eval that checks for actionability.

702
01:06:13,774 --> 01:06:17,348
You have to build a custom eval that is about your domain.

703
01:06:17,614 --> 01:06:24,100
If you are writing real evals, you're absolutely going to have to write a custom eval with a definition of good that we didn't think of.

704
01:06:25,422 --> 01:06:28,708
So that brings us to the next step, which is writing a custom eval rubric.

705
01:06:28,800 --> 01:06:32,228
This is where you make evals truly specific to your application.

706
01:06:32,720 --> 01:06:38,884
And writing a good eval is complicated, so I'm going to spend a little time talking about what makes a good eval rubric.

707
01:06:41,230 --> 01:06:43,360
We recommend four parts of a custom rubric.

708
01:06:43,534 --> 01:06:45,636
The first is to define the judge's role.

709
01:06:46,862 --> 01:06:55,280
you should spell out explicit pass and fail criteria, you should label the data with XML tags, and you should define the output choices outside the prompt

710
01:06:55,440 --> 01:06:55,684
itself.

711
01:06:56,320 --> 01:07:06,180
I'm going to add one more thing on top of those, which is actually kind of controversial amongst practitioners, which is I tend to include examples of good and bad output.

712
01:07:06,560 --> 01:07:13,188
Uh some practitioners believe that including examples will make your eval overfit.

713
01:07:14,062 --> 01:07:15,716
but let's walk through all of those.

714
01:07:16,430 --> 01:07:18,468
So the first part is defining the role.

715
01:07:20,302 --> 01:07:27,440
you'll notice this what is by now a tired trope, which is you are an expert financial analyst evaluator. That is not the important part.

716
01:07:27,918 --> 01:07:34,180
Tests show it makes some very small difference to whether or not your eval works.

717
01:07:34,480 --> 01:07:37,760
The important thing is to give it the context of what it is looking at.

718
01:07:37,920 --> 01:07:41,700
You are looking at a financial report, and what I am expecting is financial analysis.

719
01:07:42,000 --> 01:07:45,092
Those are the important pieces of context that you are giving the LLM there.

720
01:07:46,766 --> 01:07:50,948
you're also telling it that it should be that what you want is something actionable.

721
01:07:51,630 --> 01:07:53,092
So that brings us to part two.

722
01:07:53,680 --> 01:07:54,000
So

723
01:07:55,534 --> 01:07:57,440
this is where most people underinvest.

724
01:07:57,600 --> 01:08:02,148
Don't say a good response because that is an aspiration, that is not a criterion.

725
01:08:02,400 --> 01:08:04,240
A good response is helpful and accurate.

726
01:08:04,590 --> 01:08:11,364
Sounds like you're adding more detail, but you're still being just as vague because helpful and accurate don't have any meaning as far as the LLM is concerned.

727
01:08:12,320 --> 01:08:15,600
instead, you should be listing exactly what makes a report actionable.

728
01:08:15,758 --> 01:08:18,692
So list exactly what makes it actionable and not actionable.

729
01:08:19,182 --> 01:08:28,100
Every criterion should be specific and observable, something that if you were a human looking at this report, you'd be able to know whether or not it was there and you'd be able to say yes or

730
01:08:29,870 --> 01:08:34,240
So contains specific recommendations, that's something you can definitely say yes or no.

731
01:08:34,480 --> 01:08:40,100
Includes forward-looking analysis, not just historical data, that's a clear distinction that you can say yes or no to.

732
01:08:42,254 --> 01:08:47,840
And the important thing here is that each of these criteria maps to something we saw in our analysis.

733
01:08:47,920 --> 01:08:54,532
If we when we were reading the reports, we saw, we actually observed when we read our traces, or at least when I read our traces,

734
01:08:55,600 --> 01:09:00,560
that these are ways that these reports are going wrong specifically. That is not a coincidence.

735
01:09:00,720 --> 01:09:03,940
Your error analysis is what tells you what criteria to write.

736
01:09:04,238 --> 01:09:07,360
You don't come up with them from whole cloth because it's not going to occur to you.

737
01:09:07,630 --> 01:09:14,372
Where you get that data from is from reading your traces and going, oh, it didn't do that. My Axial coding said it does this specific wrong thing.

738
01:09:15,982 --> 01:09:21,156
So specific criteria produce consistent judgments, while vague criteria produce coin flips.

739
01:09:22,382 --> 01:09:30,916
The third thing you should do in your rubric is you should label the data with XML tags, especially if you are using Anthropic. Anthropic loves XML and finds it very easy to understand.

740
01:09:32,846 --> 01:09:40,944
Your rubric has variables, the input and the output, and you want to make sure that it can tell the difference between your instructions and the input and the output,

741
01:09:41,070 --> 01:09:43,332
and XML tags are an excellent way to do that.

742
01:09:44,398 --> 01:09:48,304
So put a user query tag around the input, a financial report tag around the output.

743
01:09:48,624 --> 01:09:52,864
Clear boundaries reduce the chance that the judge confuses the question with the answer.

744
01:09:52,944 --> 01:10:01,024
It is especially important if you are including examples to label the examples with XML tags, because otherwise it will go, oh, this is exactly what I'm supposed to write,

745
01:10:01,104 --> 01:10:02,788
and just spit out the example back to you.

746
01:10:05,934 --> 01:10:09,540
Which brings us to the examples. This is a part a lot of people skip.

747
01:10:09,904 --> 01:10:15,984
In my opinion, it is the part that most improves quality. LLMs are okay at following instructions.

748
01:10:16,064 --> 01:10:23,984
They are increasingly good at following instructions, but what they are really good at is copying an example that you've already given them and replacing the things

749
01:10:24,046 --> 01:10:26,144
that are relevant to the current situation.

750
01:10:26,510 --> 01:10:32,228
So if you give it an example of exactly the kind of report that you're looking for, it's going to follow that format exactly.

751
01:10:32,544 --> 01:10:36,260
That might or might not, depending on your domain, be a good idea.

752
01:10:37,070 --> 01:10:43,716
If your example is too rigid, then the LLM will always give the same report and you have reduced its scope for creativity.

753
01:10:44,384 --> 01:10:53,572
But on the other hand, if it is being too non-deterministic and your report is being crazy, then giving it an example will ground it in what exactly you are expecting.

754
01:10:55,824 --> 01:10:58,724
Here's what an actionable example looks like in the template.

755
01:10:59,104 --> 01:11:07,460
It has everything, it has percentage revenue growth, a PE ratio, it identifies a concrete risk with a number to back it up, and it gives a specific recommendation.

756
01:11:08,144 --> 01:11:09,744
That is what actionable looks like.

757
01:11:10,126 --> 01:11:11,744
Here is a non-actionable result.

758
01:11:12,144 --> 01:11:13,344
it's not wrong.

759
01:11:13,664 --> 01:11:19,556
everything it says is correct, but it doesn't say anything useful. It doesn't have any specific data, it doesn't have any specific risks.

760
01:11:19,854 --> 01:11:22,704
It just says investors should consider various factors.

761
01:11:23,024 --> 01:11:24,356
That's not a recommendation.

762
01:11:25,184 --> 01:11:31,104
so the judge now has a concrete reference for what you actually mean by the labels that you are giving it.

763
01:11:31,424 --> 01:11:39,264
and this can dramatically improve consistency, especially on the edge cases where the report would have otherwise been somewhere in between.

764
01:11:41,710 --> 01:11:51,104
and this is an important part. It is very tempting if you're writing this rubric to say, and now spit out the word correct or incorrect because that is what you want it to do.

765
01:11:51,264 --> 01:11:52,868
You do not need to do that anymore.

766
01:11:54,702 --> 01:12:03,824
the way that actually works under the hood is we have given the LLM a tool that says you call this tool when you are done, and it will call that tool with correct or incorrect when you're done.

767
01:12:03,918 --> 01:12:11,780
So you don't need to tell it what output, and in fact, telling it to spit out a specific string as output is more likely to confuse it and make your eval fail.

768
01:12:12,304 --> 01:12:18,384
So just in the UI set your choices or

769
01:12:19,598 --> 01:12:23,844
when you're creating it programmatically, set the choices and let the LLM do its thing.

770
01:12:25,504 --> 01:12:29,220
when you are coming up with these choices, it is best to keep those choices simple.

771
01:12:29,744 --> 01:12:31,844
binary is best, two clear labels.

772
01:12:32,544 --> 01:12:35,940
numeric scales, like a scale of one to five,

773
01:12:37,198 --> 01:12:42,144
they are tempting, but the LLMs are bad at them. What is the difference between a three and a four?

774
01:12:42,414 --> 01:12:45,316
You would have to write a whole bunch of rules for the LLM to know that.

775
01:12:45,664 --> 01:12:47,364
it doesn't know that automatically.

776
01:12:47,904 --> 01:12:53,188
whereas you can give it a pass fail and it will be pretty good at deciding what is a pass and what is a fail.

777
01:12:54,350 --> 01:12:57,984
so if you really need more categories, you can do like a partial result.

778
01:12:58,064 --> 01:13:02,628
You can say a 0.5 score is where it did a half assed job.

779
01:13:03,904 --> 01:13:06,500
but by default, go for a zero or one.

780
01:13:08,320 --> 01:13:11,200
One more practical tip is chain of thought for judges.

781
01:13:11,360 --> 01:13:15,972
Ask the judge to reason before it scores and have it explain its thinking first.

782
01:13:17,102 --> 01:13:19,780
This measurably improves accuracy.

783
01:13:20,240 --> 01:13:29,732
When the judge has to articulate why something is actionable or not, or why it is correct or not, it makes fewer mistakes than when it just picks a label without thinking first.

784
01:13:30,640 --> 01:13:35,520
This explanation is also very helpful for you, but it also helps the judge think better.

785
01:13:35,680 --> 01:13:37,700
Just one of those weird things about LLMs.

786
01:13:40,142 --> 01:13:45,380
So if you are coding along, now is the time to find your actionability template.

787
01:13:46,606 --> 01:13:51,524
This contains all of the example, all of the instructions that I was just talking about.

788
01:13:52,080 --> 01:13:58,692
Can you do better than me? Can you one shot your way to a perfect eval by modifying this eval template?

789
01:14:01,102 --> 01:14:05,220
As with all things in AX, you can do this via the UI as well.

790
01:14:05,742 --> 01:14:10,628
So you can create an online evaluator and you can create a new LLM as a judge.

791
01:14:10,958 --> 01:14:14,500
In this case, I've already got it, my actionability evaluator,

792
01:14:17,070 --> 01:14:21,348
which has literally exactly the same text that I just showed you in the notebook.

793
01:14:22,638 --> 01:14:26,308
And as before, I had to pick a trace scope.

794
01:14:27,520 --> 01:14:35,428
And because it's an LLM as a judge, I had to set up a template, sorry, I had to set up an LLM for it to use in the UI,

795
01:14:35,840 --> 01:14:38,560
which means that you need to give it your anthropic key again.

796
01:14:39,600 --> 01:14:44,676
And I picked my inputs and outputs via the single trace method, and then I saved it.

797
01:14:45,070 --> 01:14:55,044
One of the things that you can do with online evals is you can set them to run continuously against new incoming traces. This is something you will almost certainly do in production.

798
01:14:55,680 --> 01:15:04,356
And if your evals are expensive or you have a whole lot of traffic, you won't want to do it on all of your traffic, you can set it to serve

799
01:15:04,560 --> 01:15:06,372
to rate only a percentage.

800
01:15:06,670 --> 01:15:07,680
That's the sampling rate.

801
01:15:07,790 --> 01:15:14,596
So you can say, you know, just look at 1% of my traffic, and that will still give you a whole bunch of eval data without breaking your budget.

802
01:15:20,558 --> 01:15:23,204
so back to the code version.

803
01:15:23,950 --> 01:15:26,640
Wiring it up is just like wiring up the other

804
01:15:28,080 --> 01:15:29,700
evaluators that we've given.

805
01:15:30,080 --> 01:15:39,236
we've created a classification evaluator, we've given it a name, actionability, we've given it an LLM, we've given it that template that I just showed you, and we've given it those choices.

