01:26:39,470 --> 01:26:45,028
Precision asks, when the judge says not actionable, how often is it really not actionable?

921
01:26:45,712 --> 01:26:51,332
And recall asks, of all the reports that are actually not actionable, how many did the judge catch?

922
01:26:52,352 --> 01:26:57,412
With a tiny sample like 12 examples, these numbers are going to jump around a lot.

923
01:26:58,112 --> 01:27:04,740
But even with the 12 that I have, you can see whether the judge is in the right ballpark or completely off.

924
01:27:05,390 --> 01:27:06,832
In most scenarios,

925
01:27:09,262 --> 01:27:14,212
you want to prioritize recall. These two measurements are at odds with each other.

926
01:27:15,950 --> 01:27:23,076
If you prioritize recall, then you're going to get false positives where the judge has said something is wrong when nothing is wrong.

927
01:27:23,342 --> 01:27:27,044
That is much better than accidentally letting through things that are wrong.

928
01:27:28,302 --> 01:27:36,432
However, in some cases, when you are tuning, you will say, actually, I want precision, I want it to be absolutely right when it's right, and I don't care if it misses some things.

