01:27:36,686 --> 01:27:38,752
There are domains where that is the output that you want.

930
01:27:38,912 --> 01:27:42,180
But most of the time you want to prioritize recall.

931
01:27:46,592 --> 01:27:58,372
A few known pitfalls with using an LLM as a judge, one is position bias. If you present it with two options to judge between, the judge tends to favor whichever comes first.

932
01:27:59,438 --> 01:28:06,468
This depends on the model. Sometimes the model depends uh prefers whichever comes last, but it always has some kind of preference to position.

933
01:28:08,142 --> 01:28:09,540
There's also length bias.

934
01:28:10,032 --> 01:28:17,284
LLMs like longer responses. They tend to score those higher and say that they are better, even if the length, extra length is just filler.

935
01:28:18,112 --> 01:28:25,072
And the last one I love, which is confidence bias. The judge gets fooled by a response that sounds confident, just like humans do.

936
01:28:25,232 --> 01:28:32,516
If your LLM says things that are wrong but it says them in a really confident tone, your LLM judge is more likely to believe them.

937
01:28:34,286 --> 01:28:41,872
There's also self-preference bias. If you use the same model to do the generation as you use to do the judging, they tend to like their own output.

938
01:28:41,952 --> 01:28:45,472
So if possible, use both, say, OpenAI and Anthropic.

939
01:28:45,712 --> 01:28:51,204
Use one to do your agent and one to do the judging, and they are less likely to agree with each other.

940
01:28:54,510 --> 01:29:02,832
Self-preference bias is why I used Sonnet as a judge for output generated by Haiku, but it works even better if you use a different

