01:37:09,838 --> 01:37:15,620
as you can see, this was a data set, if you recall, where previously all of them were not actionable.

1029
01:37:16,192 --> 01:37:20,432
the eval has run, and you can see that some of them are now actionable. So we have improved.

1030
01:37:20,782 --> 01:37:27,952
And if we look at the overall score here, actually I've nailed it because I had a lot of chances to get this right.

1031
01:37:28,142 --> 01:37:35,332
I have one-shotted my prompt uh from getting being wrong 50% of the time to being right 100% of the time.

1032
01:37:35,952 --> 01:37:38,724
Uh real evals won't work like that.

1033
01:37:38,912 --> 01:37:42,112
Uh, real iteration won't run so smoothly.

1034
01:37:42,318 --> 01:37:43,552
That is why this is a graph.

1035
01:37:43,712 --> 01:37:50,992
What you're expecting to do is run experiment after experiment and slowly watch the graph climb as your evals get more and more capable.

1036
01:37:51,214 --> 01:37:53,632
That is the hill that I'm talking about that you're trying to

1037
01:37:53,632 --> 01:37:54,352
climb.

1038
01:37:58,478 --> 01:38:07,008
So the key abstraction behind experiments is that a task is just a Python function. It takes an example from the data set, it runs whatever you want, and it returns an output.

1039
01:38:07,182 --> 01:38:10,608
So AX doesn't care what the task does internally.

1040
01:38:10,734 --> 01:38:15,168
So it could run the full agent or just one component, like I said, or it could call an API.

1041
01:38:15,342 --> 01:38:19,172
As long as it takes an input and returns an output, you can run experiments on it.

1042
01:38:20,014 --> 01:38:22,948
The power of experiments is controlled comparison.

1043
01:38:23,534 --> 01:38:29,156
So you take the same inputs, the same evaluators, and the only thing that changed is the agent's prompts.

1044
01:38:29,808 --> 01:38:32,928
That means any difference in scores is attributable to your change.

1045
01:38:33,008 --> 01:38:41,060
You're not wondering, did it score higher because of my prompt change or did it score higher because the web search happens to return better results this time?

1046
01:38:41,806 --> 01:38:48,288
Well, actually, you're still wondering that a little bit but because the agent is non-deterministic, so it could have done 10 web searches instead of five web searches.

1047
01:38:48,558 --> 01:38:52,196
But you've eliminated a major source of variation, which is the test cases.

1048
01:38:55,374 --> 01:39:03,728
So, like I said, my agent has one-shotted this, which is great, but in reality, it's not going to be that

1049
01:39:03,728 --> 01:39:04,368
simple.

1050
01:39:06,126 --> 01:39:09,508
the eval iterate cycle that we're talking about here, where you go through,

1051
01:39:10,894 --> 01:39:17,316
run an experiment, change your prompt, run the experiment again over and over and over, that is where the real value lies.

1052
01:39:18,048 --> 01:39:20,288
not in the score itself, but in the cycle.

1053
01:39:20,622 --> 01:39:29,636
You run the evals, you look at the failures, you read the explanations, you identify a pattern, and you adjust your prompt, or as in this case, you get Claude to adjust your prompt for you.

1054
01:39:33,230 --> 01:39:41,732
Did it get better is a question that can only be answered by evals. You have taken the definition of good and you have codified it in a way that is incredibly useful.

1055
01:39:43,214 --> 01:39:46,980
So this allows you to move from I think it's working to I can prove it's working.

1056
01:39:48,014 --> 01:39:52,260
A practical question that comes up fast is how many samples do you need?

1057
01:39:52,848 --> 01:40:01,092
For workshop scale experiments like today, 12 to 20 examples gives you directional signal. For actual shipping decisions, you're going to want more.

1058
01:40:01,488 --> 01:40:04,228
You're going to want something like 200 to 400 is a good target.

1059
01:40:04,526 --> 01:40:10,596
But note that 200 to 400 is still like a human-sized number that you could possibly accumulate over a couple of weeks.

1060
01:40:11,168 --> 01:40:15,236
it's not tens of thousands or millions like you would use if you were training an ML model.

1061
01:40:17,134 --> 01:40:18,340
there are diminishing returns.

1062
01:40:19,278 --> 01:40:23,076
To have your margin of error, you have to quadruple your sample size.

1063
01:40:24,048 --> 01:40:25,088
So at some

1064
01:40:25,968 --> 01:40:28,420
point, you have enough signal to be able to get along

1065
01:40:31,694 --> 01:40:33,648
When you're iterating, where do you invest?

1066
01:40:34,048 --> 01:40:35,140
There's a hierarchy.

1067
01:40:36,128 --> 01:40:44,848
data quality fixes have the highest impact. If your agent is searching the wrong sources or your knowledge base has stale information, then no amount of prompt iteration is going to help.

1068
01:40:45,008 --> 01:40:46,244
You should fix the data first.

1069
01:40:47,008 --> 01:40:48,688
Then prompting improvements are next.

1070
01:40:48,974 --> 01:40:58,528
Few shot examples, explicit instructions, the kind of stuff that I mentioned today, constraints on what the agent should and shouldn't do, these are often the highest ROI change

1071
01:40:58,862 --> 01:40:59,908
to your agent.

1072
01:41:02,190 --> 01:41:03,568
Model selection comes third.

1073
01:41:03,728 --> 01:41:11,332
Sometimes a more capable model solves problems that prompting can't, but it will also cost more and be slower, so that is a trade-off that you have to make.

1074
01:41:11,968 --> 01:41:15,140
The thing to not spend a lot of time on is hyperparameter tuning.

1075
01:41:15,248 --> 01:41:20,068
Things like temperature and top P, it's very tempting to just go for them because they're easy to tweak.

1076
01:41:20,688 --> 01:41:26,067
They very seldom make a high-leverage change like prompting would or improving the quality of your data would.

1077
01:41:27,214 --> 01:41:29,924
So you can try them, but they should be the last thing you try.

1078
01:41:31,278 --> 01:41:35,876
A practice worth knowing is eval-driven development. Write the eval before you build the feature.

1079
01:41:36,974 --> 01:41:44,516
If you want your agent to always verify customer identity before it does something, before processing a refund, for instance, then write the eval that checks for that first.

1080
01:41:44,910 --> 01:41:49,168
That gives you a clear, measurable definition of what good looks like, of what done looks like.

1081
01:41:49,358 --> 01:41:51,408
Then you can build a feature

1082
01:41:52,366 --> 01:41:56,356
until the eval passes. This is the same philosophy as test driven development.

1083
01:41:59,808 --> 01:42:00,128
And

1084
01:42:00,208 --> 01:42:06,688
something I love about eval-driven development is that the people closest to product requirements are best positioned to define success.

1085
01:42:06,848 --> 01:42:13,604
So product managers, customer service people, even salespeople can contribute to eval tasks.

1086
01:42:13,728 --> 01:42:16,804
They can tell you what looks good and what doesn't look good in practice.

1087
01:42:17,568 --> 01:42:25,648
They don't need to write code, they just need to describe in natural language what good looks like to them, and you can boil that down into your eval

1088
01:42:25,808 --> 01:42:26,116
rubric.

1089
01:42:30,062 --> 01:42:31,088
And so far

1090
01:42:32,494 --> 01:42:35,728
I've shown you how to do things both offline and online.

1091
01:42:37,326 --> 01:42:39,172
We've been doing everything in a notebook,

1092
01:42:40,366 --> 01:42:48,068
but you're not really done when your dev experiment passes, you're done when your agent keeps performing in production on traffic that you've never seen.

