01:42:49,966 --> 01:42:55,044
And it's worth spending a couple of minutes talking about that, even though we're going to stick in the notebook

1094
01:42:56,558 --> 01:42:57,412
for this work session.

1095
01:42:59,150 --> 01:43:05,728
the first thing is online evals. like I said, and like I showed you earlier, online evals are exactly what they sound like.

1096
01:43:05,838 --> 01:43:14,448
They take the evaluators that you wrote today, the ticker check, the faithfulness, the actionability, and run them automatically on incoming production traces.

1097
01:43:14,542 --> 01:43:16,548
The same evals running on new data.

1098
01:43:18,094 --> 01:43:22,048
I showed you how to run this on a trace. AX lets you do this at three scopes.

1099
01:43:22,208 --> 01:43:30,048
You can run your evals at the span level, so you can say this particular LLM call, this particular tool call, run a trace on it every single time.

1100
01:43:30,382 --> 01:43:38,948
Sorry, run an eval on it every single time, or you can run it at the trace level where it has the full tree to look at and it can evaluate everything from start to finish.

1101
01:43:40,558 --> 01:43:43,012
you pick the scope that matches the question that you're asking.

1102
01:43:44,686 --> 01:43:48,128
and you don't need to run evals on every single prediction trace, I uh

1103
01:43:49,070 --> 01:43:50,180
production trace rather.

1104
01:43:50,542 --> 01:43:55,328
Um LLM judges cost money, and at that production scale, those costs can add up.

1105
01:43:55,408 --> 01:44:04,688
So you sample 10% of your traffic or 1% of your traffic, and that gives you a directional signal without you having to run an expensive LLM as a judge on every single iteration,

1106
01:44:04,848 --> 01:44:07,332
every single instance of your application.

1107
01:44:11,278 --> 01:44:20,420
if you don't want to write your evals by hand, we have good news for you, which is that we have built an agent into AX itself. It is called Alex.

1108
01:44:21,966 --> 01:44:26,357
You can describe your eval in plain English, or you can get your non-technical

1109
01:44:27,502 --> 01:44:28,288
colleagues to

1110
01:44:29,248 --> 01:44:34,500
describe the definition of good in plain English. and Alex will actually manipulate the UI for you.

1111
01:44:35,008 --> 01:44:40,036
create an eval, write the rubric, set all the scores correctly, get all the configuration correct.

1112
01:44:40,206 --> 01:44:41,668
and then just tell you that it's done.

1113
01:44:42,926 --> 01:44:45,077
this is especially useful for non-engineers.

1114
01:44:47,557 --> 01:44:56,868
And the full loop in production looks like this: you go from application which produces traces to online evals which grade those traces and produce labels and explanations.

1115
01:44:57,230 --> 01:45:00,932
The eval labels feed into monitors, which we didn't cover today.

1116
01:45:01,998 --> 01:45:04,612
the monitors can alert your team when something goes wrong.

1117
01:45:05,742 --> 01:45:09,540
The team can investigate, find the failing traces, save them as a regression data set,

1118
01:45:10,766 --> 01:45:14,084
improve the agent, run an experiment to verify, and ship the fix.

1119
01:45:14,478 --> 01:45:15,908
And then the loop can run again.

1120
01:45:17,968 --> 01:45:24,448
As new production traffic accumulates, you will accumulate new failure modes, you will accumulate new edge cases.

1121
01:45:24,768 --> 01:45:30,244
you're always going to be iterating on your evals and iterating on your production monitoring.

1122
01:45:33,006 --> 01:45:38,160
But it is a payoff that compounds. Every failure that your online evals catch becomes a new test case.

1123
01:45:38,318 --> 01:45:41,796
You save it to a data set, and now it's a regression test for your next round of changes.

1124
01:45:42,254 --> 01:45:45,924
Over time, this creates a data set that is unique to your application.

1125
01:45:46,640 --> 01:45:51,076
So your specific failure modes, your specific users, your specific quality bar.

1126
01:45:51,280 --> 01:45:56,868
Nobody else has that data, and that is a competitive advantage that grows every single time your agent runs.

1127
01:45:59,374 --> 01:46:05,520
There's one last pattern that I want to leave you with, which is the most powerful one I know. We did the simplest version today.

1128
01:46:05,742 --> 01:46:09,252
We fed the judge's explanations back to Claude and had it rewrite two prompts.

1129
01:46:09,440 --> 01:46:13,636
This works beautifully for two prompts, but a real system isn't two prompts.

1130
01:46:13,838 --> 01:46:18,720
It's prompts scattered across many files, retrieval settings, tool definitions all tangled together.

1131
01:46:18,880 --> 01:46:25,572
Rewriting that isn't a single API call, it's a job for a coding agent that can see your entire repository.

1132
01:46:27,022 --> 01:46:36,772
So this is the pattern. You want to export your failing traces from AX along with their explanations and hand the whole batch to Cloud Code or Cursor or another coding agent as context.

1133
01:46:37,920 --> 01:46:47,200
This is where the Arise skills plugin that I mentioned at the beginning works really well because you don't have because you don't have to know how to call our API,

1134
01:46:47,360 --> 01:46:49,120
you don't have to know how to do any of that stuff.

1135
01:46:49,358 --> 01:46:57,252
You give it the skills and then tell Cloud Code, hey, pull down some traces, see what's wrong, and make make my application better, and it works.

1136
01:47:00,558 --> 01:47:02,880
There should be two guardrails on that approach first, however.

1137
01:47:03,040 --> 01:47:06,160
First, you should feed it your requirements too, and not just the failure explanations.

1138
01:47:06,240 --> 01:47:10,720
So the goal isn't make the evals pass because otherwise it will cheat.

1139
01:47:10,800 --> 01:47:15,684
It will include all of your test data into its eval and automatically pass all of your evals.

1140
01:47:15,982 --> 01:47:17,520
So you have to make uh

1141
01:47:19,854 --> 01:47:28,320
your you have to make sure that your requirements to your cloud code are clear that you don't want it just to pass the evals, you want it to do this specific thing, which then makes the evals

1142
01:47:28,560 --> 01:47:28,880
pass.

1143
01:47:30,254 --> 01:47:33,840
and second, you should tell it to find themes and not chase individual failures.

1144
01:47:34,067 --> 01:47:42,080
It's very tempting for Cloud Code to look at one particular failed trace and write a whole bunch of code to fix that particular failed trace when there are actually 10 failing traces that are

1145
01:47:42,080 --> 01:47:45,124
all the same cause that it should be focused on instead.

1146
01:47:46,830 --> 01:47:47,440
I'm not going to do

1147
01:47:48,400 --> 01:47:50,340
all of that, because I only have 12 minutes left.

1148
01:47:52,960 --> 01:47:53,280
But

1149
01:47:55,086 --> 01:47:58,884
once you have evals running, this is one of the highest leverage things that you can do with them.

1150
01:48:01,038 --> 01:48:03,600
So let's step back and look at the whole shape of it.

1151
01:48:03,920 --> 01:48:08,000
production produces traces, online evals grade them and produce explanations.

1152
01:48:08,206 --> 01:48:14,404
A coding agent reads the explanations and proposes fixes, and experiments verify that the fixes work, and then you ship.

1153
01:48:15,022 --> 01:48:20,260
The production traffic that comes back gets graded by the same online evals that started the loop.

1154
01:48:21,230 --> 01:48:24,484
and this is the software development lifecycle closing in on itself.

1155
01:48:25,120 --> 01:48:30,880
your AI software is helping you improve your AI software, and AX is the substrate that ties it all together.

1156
01:48:31,040 --> 01:48:36,868
Your traces, the evals, the explanations, the data sets, the experiments, the online evals, and the monitors.

