01:49:29,200 --> 01:49:34,276
15 minutes of reading real outputs will teach you more about your application than an hour of building dashboards.

1169
01:49:34,766 --> 01:49:40,036
Then write one code eval. Code evals are easier than LLM evals, and they're faster and they're cheaper.

1170
01:49:41,038 --> 01:49:42,628
And make it for the thing that matters most.

1171
01:49:42,798 --> 01:49:49,732
So correctness, faithfulness, whatever fits your use case. Run them, look at the results, see what patterns emerge, and build from there.

1172
01:49:51,054 --> 01:49:52,480
Evals are infrastructure.

1173
01:49:52,622 --> 01:49:59,360
They are not an afterthought. Some teams create evals at the very start of development, others add them later once they are at scale.

1174
01:49:59,680 --> 01:50:04,388
But either way, the important thing is that you treat evals as a core part of your system.

1175
01:50:04,942 --> 01:50:10,480
They should be as routine as unit tests, and the value compounds, but only if you keep investing.

1176
01:50:10,640 --> 01:50:18,080
